\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.8--85.9\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across seven generalization axes (four below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} evaluates bagging (RF) and gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) on the same 19 features; per-cell spread is $\leq$0.4pp, establishing a feature-determined ceiling.
On Pythia nucleus, CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match the within-cell RF of Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} within 0.6\,pp (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected); all feature-based methods significantly outperform CE-MLP (74.9\%, $p<0.001$) and logistic regression (75.0\%).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); two zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%; each 500 samples, prompt in Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) also fail, confirming that the depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text alone (43\,pp gap vs.\ RF at 78.7\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Classifier convergence: 4-class accuracy (\%) across three atlas cells. RF (bagging), XGBoost and LightGBM (gradient boosting) operate on the same 19 features; $\Delta_{\max}$: largest per-cell spread. Chance = 25\%. RF values differ from Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} by $\leq$0.2pp due to matched-fold evaluation. 5-fold cross-validation standard deviation $\leq$ 0.9\,pp across all settings.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Cell & RF & XGB & LGBM & $\Delta_{\max}$ \\
\midrule
Pythia, nuc. & 78.8 & 79.0 & 78.9 & 0.2 \\
GPT-2~XL, $\tau$ & 85.8 & 86.2 & 86.2 & 0.4 \\
Pythia, top-$k$ & 68.9 & 68.9 & 68.7 & 0.2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Convergence confirms the signal resides in the features, not the classifier; this ceiling extends to cross-model transfer, where XGBoost (67.2\%), ordinal MLP (66.7\%), and prototypical networks (66.6\%) all match the RF baseline (66.4\%) within 0.8\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}).
A feature-group ablation further localizes the signal: the 9 perplexity features alone recover 66.0\% ($-$0.5\,pp vs.\ full), while repetition/diversity features alone reach only 53.0\% ($-$13.6\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation}), confirming perplexity degradation as the dominant depth marker.
Raw perplexity (mean/median) performs at chance (25.1\%); the strongest single feature (\texttt{p90\_ppl}) achieves 58.3\% ($-$8.3\,pp vs.\ full), confirming that ordinal depth estimation requires multi-dimensional characterization of the perplexity distribution rather than a single threshold.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified; errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer (seed std $= 0.06$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:rf_seed}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.9\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%).}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline (d1--d3 balanced mean 55.4\%).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%); excluding d0, d1--d3 accuracy drops to 45.9\% (chance = 33\%).
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature inverts the d1/d2 boundary (d1v2 AUC $<$ 0.5); per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers it but leaves a residual gap (Limitations).
A multi-temperature ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:multi_tau}) confirms within-$\tau$ signal for $\tau \leq 0.9$ but collapse at $\tau{=}1.1$; end-to-end PLMs lag RF by 20pp (Appendix~\ref{app:e2e}).

